// The memory games' practice content in English. Translated by Claude Code from memory_practice.ink; it
// follows that file line for line (the parity test in Story/Tests checks it). A choice tagged #good is the good answer.

// Choose: young Teta buys cardamom at the souk in 1974 and haggles.
=== practice_choose ===
Good morning, uncle. How much is a kilo of cardamom today? #teta_young
Five lira, ma'am. First-grade cardamom. #seller_1974
* [“It was four yesterday, uncle.” #good]
    By God, the shipment went up… but for you, four and a half. #seller_1974
* [“Fine, five.” #weak]
    God bless you. #seller_1974
-
Anything else? I have fresh cinnamon from Damascus. #seller_1974
* [“If you give me the cinnamon with the cardamom for one price, I'll take both.” #good]
    You drive a hard bargain, ma'am! Fine, fine. #seller_1974
* [“No, just the cardamom.” #weak]
    As you like. #seller_1974
-
Did you know green cardamom costs more than white? #seller_1974
* [“I know, and this green is half white.” #good]
    You have a good eye… let me pick for you from the other box. #seller_1974
* [“Really? I didn't know.” #weak]
    That's how you learn. #seller_1974
-
Who's all this cardamom for? #seller_1974
* [“For my café. I'm opening it next month in Jabal Amman.” #good]
    A café?! Then I'll be your customer, and you'll be mine. #seller_1974
* [“For me.” #weak]
    Enjoy it. #seller_1974
-
If you take two kilos every month, I'll give you a special price. #seller_1974
* [“Two kilos, and you deliver them to the café.” #good]
    Deal. But the first cup is on you. #seller_1974
* [“Let's see next month.” #weak]
    The door's always open. #seller_1974
-
Come on, four and a half and we're done? #seller_1974
* [“Four, and I'll bring you the first cup myself.” #good]
    Four! But the cup has to be without sugar. #seller_1974
* [“Four and a half.” #weak]
    Here you are, ma'am. #seller_1974
-
-> END

// Reassemble: the strips of a torn note, in their right order. Each line is one strip.
=== practice_letter ===
Mother, #letter
The café opened today, and every chair was taken. #letter
Abu Fadi was the first customer; he ordered a coffee and a smile. #letter
The seller at the souk brings the cardamom himself now. #letter
The window I put in the corner lets in the afternoon sun. #letter
I'll wait for you on Friday; the first cup is yours. #letter
— Your daughter #letter_signature
-> END
